# endoflife.dadl -- endoflife.date REST API (v1) for ToolMesh
# endoflife.date is a community-maintained catalog of support lifecycles for
# about 490 products: operating systems, languages, databases, frameworks,
# server software, cloud services and devices. For every product it lists the
# release cycles with release date, LTS flag, end of active support, end of
# life, end of extended support and the newest version within each cycle. It
# answers "is this version still supported, until when, and is there a newer
# patch or a newer cycle?" without reading release notes.
#
# Domain Notes for LLM consumers:
# - PUBLIC, KEY-FREE, READ-ONLY API. No account, key or token. The auth block
#   below is the ToolMesh placeholder for APIs without authentication
#   (credential "none"). The API is static JSON served from a CDN; the data is
#   regenerated whenever the project's repository changes (several times a
#   day), so generated_at moves often while a product's last_modified says
#   when its own data changed. No rate limit is published; the API documents
#   HTTP 429 with a Retry-After header.
# - TWELVE ENDPOINTS under /api/v1: the index (used here as the health check)
#   and eleven GETs, all covered: products (summary list, full dump, one
#   product), releases (one cycle, the first-listed cycle), categories and
#   tags (lists and their products), identifiers (types and the id-to-product
#   map).
# - VOCABULARY: API v1 calls a release cycle a "release" (path
#   /products/{product}/releases/{release}); the website and API v0 call it a
#   "cycle". A release cycle such as Ubuntu "22.04", PostgreSQL "16" or
#   Python "3.12" groups many versions; its "latest" is the newest version
#   inside the cycle ("22.04.5", "16.4", "3.12.7"). Tool names keep the API
#   word; descriptions say "release cycle".
# - PRODUCT NAMES are slugs: "ubuntu", "postgresql", "nodejs",
#   "hashicorp-vault", "alpine-linux", "docker-engine". Lookups are
#   case-insensitive. Aliases ("postgres", "node", "vault", "alpine",
#   "golang") answer with a 301 redirect to the canonical product, which
#   ToolMesh follows, so get_product returns the canonical name. An unknown
#   name is HTTP 404 with an HTML body (no JSON error message). Find slugs
#   with list_products (name, label, aliases) or, for a purl, CPE or repology
#   id, with list_identifiers.
# - RELEASE NAMES are the cycle names exactly as the product lists them
#   ("22.04", "16", "3.12", "1.27", "11-26h2-e", "6+"). They are not
#   versions: "22.04.5" or "16.4" return 404. Map an installed version to its
#   cycle by the longest cycle name that is a prefix of the version at a
#   ".", "-", "_" or "+" boundary (check_release_status does this). Some
#   products name cycles differently from the version string a host reports
#   (Java "8" vs "1.8.0_392"); pass the cycle explicitly there.
# - "LATEST" in /releases/latest is the FIRST cycle in the product's list:
#   usually the newest by release date, but a few products list an umbrella
#   cycle first (jenkins and log4j list "2"). It is never "the newest LTS":
#   for Ubuntu it is the newest interim release. Filter get_product's
#   releases by isLts for the newest LTS.
# - LIFECYCLE FIELDS (v1 splits the v0 union fields into boolean + date):
#   isLts/ltsFrom; isEoas/eoasFrom = end of ACTIVE support (bug fixes stop,
#   security fixes may continue); isEol/eolFrom = end of life (no more
#   security fixes in standard support); isEoes/eoesFrom = end of EXTENDED
#   support, usually paid (Ubuntu ESM, RHEL ELS, Windows ESU);
#   isDiscontinued/discontinuedFrom (hardware); isMaintained = any level of
#   support left, extended support included. The booleans reflect the day
#   the data was generated. Fields that do not apply are ABSENT (isEoas and
#   eoasFrom without an active-support phase, isEoes and eoesFrom without an
#   extended phase, isDiscontinued for software); isEoes is null when a cycle
#   is not eligible for extended support. A null date means "not known", not
#   "never": hundreds of cycles are isEol: true with eolFrom null. A cycle can
#   be isEol: true and isMaintained: true at once (only extended support
#   left).
# - PHASE LABELS: labels.eoas, labels.eol and labels.eoes on a product say
#   what each phase means for that vendor ("Hardware & Maintenance",
#   "Maintenance & Security Support", "Expanded Security Maintenance").
# - LATEST VERSION is {name, date, link} or null (about a quarter of all
#   cycles have none, mostly devices and services). name is in the vendor's
#   format and can be a word ("N/A", "unknown", "Patch 9"): compare versions
#   numerically, never as strings. For operating systems latest is the
#   newest point release (installation media); a host patched through its
#   package manager can report only the cycle ("22.04").
# - CUSTOM FIELDS: some products add per-cycle fields under custom
#   (supportedPhpVersions, recommendedReplacement,
#   supportedKubernetesVersions, chromeVersion, ...); null when the product
#   defines none.
# - IDENTIFIERS map external ids to products: purl (~1,000, e.g.
#   pkg:docker/library/postgres -> postgresql), cpe (CPE 2.2 and 2.3 names
#   without version, e.g. cpe:2.3:o:canonical:ubuntu_linux -> ubuntu) and
#   repology project names. The ids carry no version; strip "@version",
#   qualifiers ("?...") and subpath ("#...") before matching, and percent-
#   decode (pkg:npm/%40scope/name). Coverage is partial: many products and
#   images have no purl (e.g. pkg:docker/library/nginx is not listed while
#   the product nginx exists).
# - SIZES: list_products ~490 summaries (~60 KB after projection);
#   get_product 2-20 KB; list_identifiers for purl ~1,000 entries (~75 KB
#   after projection); /products/full is a dump of nearly all data (~3 MB
#   raw, ~2 MB after the projection here) for scans inside code only.
# - ERRORS: a 404 carries the semantic code not_found and, as its message,
#   the site's whole HTML error page (~800 bytes), because the API has no
#   JSON error body. Inside code a failed call comes back as that error
#   text; test for an object before reading fields.
# - CATEGORIES AND TAGS: every product has exactly one category (os, lang,
#   database, framework, server-app, app, service, device, standard; new
#   values may appear), which is also one of its tags. Tags group vendors
#   and kinds ("linux-distribution", "hashicorp", "java-distribution",
#   "web-server"). Unknown names are HTTP 404.
# - API v0 (/api/{product}.json, /api/{product}/{cycle}.json,
#   /api/all.json) is the legacy format with union fields (eol: date or
#   false) that the project plans to retire; this file covers v1 only.
# - Verified against the live API (2026-11, schema_version 1.2.1).

spec: "https://dadl.ai/spec/dadl-spec-v0.2.md"

credits:
  - "Dunkel Cloud GmbH -- maintainer"

source_name: "endoflife.date API v1"
source_url: "https://endoflife.date/docs/api/v1/"
date: "2026-11-25"

backend:
  name: endoflife
  type: rest
  version: "1.0"
  base_url: "https://endoflife.date/api/v1"
  description: "endoflife.date API v1 (public, no key): end-of-life dates and support lifecycles of ~490 products (operating systems, languages, databases, frameworks, server software, cloud services, devices) -- release cycles with LTS flag, end of active support, end of life, extended support and the newest version per cycle; lookup by name, alias, category, tag, purl or CPE; batch check of installed versions."

  # No authentication -- endoflife.date is fully public. ToolMesh requires an
  # auth block; the credential "none" is its placeholder for APIs without
  # auth: it is never looked up and no header or query parameter is injected.
  auth:
    type: apikey
    credential: none
    inject_into: header

  # Health: there is no credential to verify, so the check covers
  # reachability and the response format only. The API index (~300 bytes)
  # is the cheapest side-effect-free call.
  health:
    method: GET
    path: /
    expect_path: "$.result"
    timeout: 5s

  defaults:
    headers:
      Accept: application/json
    errors:
      format: json
      message_path: "$.message"   # error pages are HTML; the status code is what counts
      retry_on: [429, 500, 502, 503, 504]
      terminal: [400, 401, 403, 404]
      retry_strategy:
        max_retries: 3
        backoff: exponential
        initial_delay: 1s
      map:
        404: not_found
        429: rate_limited
    response:
      result_path: "$"
      allow_jq_override: true

  types:
    ProductSummary:
      type: object
      properties:
        name: { type: string, description: "Product slug taken by every other tool" }
        label: { type: string, description: "Display name" }
        aliases: { type: array, items: { type: string }, description: "Other accepted names (redirect to name)" }
        category: { type: string, description: "os | lang | database | framework | server-app | app | service | device | standard (new values may appear)" }
        tags: { type: array, items: { type: string } }
      required: [name]
    LatestVersion:
      type: object
      description: "Newest version inside a release cycle."
      properties:
        name: { type: string, description: "Version in the vendor's format; can be a word such as N/A" }
        date: { type: string, description: "Release date (YYYY-MM-DD) or null" }
        link: { type: string, description: "Changelog or release notes, or null" }
    Release:
      type: object
      description: "One release cycle. Fields that do not apply to the product are absent; a null date means unknown."
      properties:
        name: { type: string, description: "Cycle name, e.g. 22.04, 16, 3.12" }
        codename: { type: string }
        label: { type: string }
        releaseDate: { type: string, description: "YYYY-MM-DD" }
        isLts: { type: boolean }
        ltsFrom: { type: string }
        isEoas: { type: boolean, description: "Active support over" }
        eoasFrom: { type: string }
        isEol: { type: boolean, description: "End of life reached" }
        eolFrom: { type: string }
        isEoes: { type: boolean, description: "Extended support over (null = not eligible)" }
        eoesFrom: { type: string }
        isDiscontinued: { type: boolean }
        discontinuedFrom: { type: string }
        isMaintained: { type: boolean, description: "Any level of support left, extended support included" }
        latest: LatestVersion
        custom: { type: object, description: "Product-specific per-cycle fields, or null" }
      required: [name]
    Identifier:
      type: object
      properties:
        type: { type: string, description: "purl | cpe | repology" }
        id: { type: string }
    Product:
      type: object
      properties:
        name: { type: string }
        label: { type: string }
        aliases: { type: array, items: { type: string } }
        category: { type: string }
        tags: { type: array, items: { type: string } }
        versionCommand: { type: string, description: "Shell command that prints the installed version, or null" }
        identifiers: { type: array, items: Identifier }
        labels: { type: object, description: "Phase names: eoas, eol, eoes, discontinued" }
        links: { type: object, description: "html (product page), releasePolicy, icon" }
        releases: { type: array, items: Release }
        last_modified: { type: string, description: "When this product's data last changed" }
      required: [name, releases]
    ProductReleases:
      type: object
      properties:
        name: { type: string }
        label: { type: string }
        aliases: { type: array, items: { type: string } }
        category: { type: string }
        tags: { type: array, items: { type: string } }
        releases: { type: array, items: Release, description: "Without label, custom and latest.link" }
      required: [name, releases]
    IdentifierRef:
      type: object
      properties:
        identifier: { type: string, description: "purl, CPE name or repology project, without version" }
        product: { type: string, description: "Product slug" }
      required: [identifier, product]

  tools:

    # =========================================================================
    # Products -- the catalog and one product with all its release cycles.
    # =========================================================================

    list_products:
      method: GET
      path: /products
      access: read
      description: >
        List all products on endoflife.date (about 490; ~60 KB): name (the
        slug every other tool takes), label (display name), aliases (other
        accepted names, e.g. "postgres" for postgresql, "vault" for
        hashicorp-vault, "node" for nodejs), category (os, lang, database,
        framework, server-app, app, service, device, standard) and tags.
        Summaries only, no release cycles -- call get_product for those. Use it
        to find the slug before get_product or check_release_status; inside
        code, filter by name, label and aliases rather than returning the whole
        list. Narrower lists: list_category_products, list_tag_products.
      returns:
        type: array
        items: ProductSummary
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        transform: |
          [(.result // [])[] | {name, label, aliases, category, tags}]
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

    list_products_full:
      method: GET
      path: /products/full
      access: read
      description: >
        Dump of ALL products with ALL their release cycles (about 490 products
        and 9,500 cycles; ~3 MB raw, ~2 MB after projection). Meant for scans
        across products INSIDE CODE -- e.g. "which cycles reach end of life in
        the next 90 days" -- never as a direct result. Each product: name,
        label, aliases, category, tags and releases[] with the fields of
        get_release except label, custom and latest.link (dropped here; call
        get_product for them). Lifecycle fields: isLts, isEoas/eoasFrom (end of
        active support), isEol/eolFrom (end of life), isEoes/eoesFrom (end of
        extended support), isMaintained; absent fields do not apply, null dates
        are unknown. For one or a few products call get_product instead.
      returns:
        type: array
        items: ProductReleases
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        transform: |
          [(.result // [])[] | {
            name, label, aliases, category, tags,
            releases: [(.releases // [])[]
              | del(.label, .custom)
              | if .latest == null then . else .latest |= {name, date} end]
          }]
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

    get_product:
      method: GET
      path: /products/{product}
      access: read
      description: >
        Get one product with ALL its release cycles (GET /products/{product}).
        Returns name, label, aliases, category, tags, versionCommand (shell
        command that prints the installed version), identifiers ([{type, id}]
        with purl, cpe, repology), labels (what the phases mean for this
        vendor: eoas, eol, eoes, discontinued), links (html product page,
        releasePolicy, icon), last_modified and releases[], usually newest
        first. Each release cycle: name ("22.04", "16", "3.12"), codename,
        label, releaseDate, isLts/ltsFrom, isEoas/eoasFrom (end of ACTIVE
        support), isEol/eolFrom (END OF LIFE), isEoes/eoesFrom (end of EXTENDED,
        usually paid support; isEoes null = not eligible), isDiscontinued/
        discontinuedFrom (hardware), isMaintained (any support left, extended
        included), latest ({name, date, link} of the newest version in the
        cycle, or null; name may be a word like "N/A") and custom
        (product-specific fields or null). Fields that do not apply are ABSENT;
        a null date means unknown, not never. isEol true with isMaintained true
        means only extended support is left. Accepts aliases ("postgres") and
        any letter case; an unknown name is HTTP 404 (code not_found, the
        message is the site's HTML error page).
      params:
        product:
          type: string
          in: path
          required: true
          description: "Product slug or alias, case-insensitive (e.g. 'ubuntu', 'postgresql', 'postgres', 'nodejs', 'hashicorp-vault', 'alpine-linux'); not a display label."
      returns: Product
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        transform: |
          (.result // {}) + {last_modified}
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

    # =========================================================================
    # Releases -- one release cycle of a product.
    # =========================================================================

    get_release:
      method: GET
      path: /products/{product}/releases/{release}
      access: read
      description: >
        Get one release cycle of a product (GET
        /products/{product}/releases/{release}): name, codename, label,
        releaseDate, isLts/ltsFrom, isEoas/eoasFrom (end of active support),
        isEol/eolFrom (end of life), isEoes/eoesFrom (end of extended support),
        isDiscontinued/discontinuedFrom, isMaintained, latest ({name, date,
        link} of the newest version in the cycle, or null) and custom. release
        is the CYCLE NAME exactly as get_product lists it ("22.04", "16",
        "3.12", "1.27"), NOT a full version: "22.04.5" or "16.4" is HTTP 404.
        To map an installed version to its cycle use check_release_status.
        Fields that do not apply are absent; a null date means unknown.
      params:
        product:
          type: string
          in: path
          required: true
          description: "Product slug or alias, case-insensitive (e.g. 'ubuntu', 'postgresql')."
        release:
          type: string
          in: path
          required: true
          description: "Release cycle name as listed in get_product releases[].name (e.g. '22.04', '16', '3.12'), not a full version."
      returns: Release
      response:
        result_path: "$.result"
      pagination: none

    get_release_latest:
      method: GET
      path: /products/{product}/releases/latest
      access: read
      description: >
        Get the FIRST release cycle in the product's list (GET
        /products/{product}/releases/latest), same fields as get_release. This
        is usually the newest cycle by release date, but not always: a few
        products list an umbrella cycle first (jenkins and log4j return "2"),
        and it is never "the newest LTS" (for Ubuntu it is the newest interim
        release). For the newest LTS or a date-based choice, call get_product
        and filter releases (isLts, releaseDate).
      params:
        product:
          type: string
          in: path
          required: true
          description: "Product slug or alias, case-insensitive (e.g. 'ubuntu', 'nodejs')."
      returns: Release
      response:
        result_path: "$.result"
      pagination: none

    # =========================================================================
    # Categories and tags -- browse products by kind or vendor.
    # =========================================================================

    list_categories:
      method: GET
      path: /categories
      access: read
      description: >
        List the category names (GET /categories): os, lang, database,
        framework, server-app, app, service, device, standard (new values may
        appear). Every product has exactly one category. Returns an array of
        names; pass one to list_category_products.
      returns:
        type: array
        items: { type: string }
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        transform: |
          [(.result // [])[] | .name]
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

    list_category_products:
      method: GET
      path: /categories/{category}
      access: read
      description: >
        List the products of one category (GET /categories/{category}), as
        summaries {name, label, aliases, category, tags} like list_products
        (no release cycles). An unknown category is HTTP 404.
      params:
        category:
          type: string
          in: path
          required: true
          description: "Category name from list_categories (e.g. 'os', 'database', 'lang', 'server-app')."
      returns:
        type: array
        items: ProductSummary
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        transform: |
          [(.result // [])[] | {name, label, aliases, category, tags}]
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

    list_tags:
      method: GET
      path: /tags
      access: read
      description: >
        List the tag names (GET /tags, about 90): the categories plus vendor
        and kind tags such as "linux-distribution", "web-server",
        "java-distribution", "hashicorp", "microsoft". Returns an array of
        names; pass one to list_tag_products.
      returns:
        type: array
        items: { type: string }
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        transform: |
          [(.result // [])[] | .name]
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

    list_tag_products:
      method: GET
      path: /tags/{tag}
      access: read
      description: >
        List the products carrying one tag (GET /tags/{tag}), as summaries
        {name, label, aliases, category, tags} like list_products (no release
        cycles). An unknown tag is HTTP 404 -- take names from list_tags.
      params:
        tag:
          type: string
          in: path
          required: true
          description: "Tag name from list_tags (e.g. 'linux-distribution', 'web-server', 'hashicorp')."
      returns:
        type: array
        items: ProductSummary
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        transform: |
          [(.result // [])[] | {name, label, aliases, category, tags}]
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

    # =========================================================================
    # Identifiers -- map purl, CPE and repology ids to products.
    # =========================================================================

    list_identifier_types:
      method: GET
      path: /identifiers
      access: read
      description: >
        List the identifier types endoflife.date maps to products (GET
        /identifiers): purl, cpe, repology. Returns an array of names; pass one
        to list_identifiers.
      returns:
        type: array
        items: { type: string }
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        transform: |
          [(.result // [])[] | .name]
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

    list_identifiers:
      method: GET
      path: /identifiers/{identifier_type}
      access: read
      description: >
        List every known identifier of one type with the product it belongs to
        (GET /identifiers/{identifier_type}), as [{identifier, product}] where
        product is the slug for get_product. purl: about 1,000 package URLs
        WITHOUT version (pkg:docker/library/postgres -> postgresql,
        pkg:github/traefik/traefik -> traefik, pkg:npm/%40scope/name stays
        percent-encoded); cpe: CPE 2.2 and 2.3 names without version
        (cpe:2.3:o:canonical:ubuntu_linux -> ubuntu); repology: project names.
        To look up an inventory purl, strip "@version", "?qualifiers" and
        "#subpath" first (check_release_status does this). Coverage is partial:
        a missing purl does not mean the product is unknown (nginx exists, but
        pkg:docker/library/nginx is not listed) -- fall back to list_products.
      params:
        identifier_type:
          type: string
          in: path
          required: true
          description: "Identifier type: 'purl', 'cpe' or 'repology' (see list_identifier_types)."
      returns:
        type: array
        items: IdentifierRef
      response:
        result_path: "$"
        transform: |
          [(.result // [])[] | {identifier, product: .product.name}]
        allow_jq_override: true
      pagination: none

  # ===========================================================================
  # Composites
  # ===========================================================================
  composites:

    check_release_status:
      access: read
      description: >
        Support-status check for a list of installed products: resolves each
        item to a product (slug, alias or purl), maps the version to its
        release cycle and reports end-of-life, extended support, the newest
        patch in the cycle and newer cycles. Input items: {product, version}
        or {purl} with the version in the purl ("pkg:docker/library/postgres@16.4")
        or in version; release names the cycle explicitly when the version
        string does not start with the cycle name (Java "1.8.0_392" -> "8").
        A leading "v" is ignored. The cycle is the longest cycle name that
        prefixes the version at a ".", "-", "_", "+" or space boundary. Returns
        {checked, status_counts, results: [...]} in input order; each result
        has index, query, status, product, product_label, release, codename,
        is_lts, release_date, is_eoas/eoas_from, is_eol/eol_from/days_to_eol,
        is_eoes/eoes_from, is_maintained, installed_version,
        latest_in_release/latest_date, update_available, newest_release,
        newer_release_available, newest_lts_release and error (keys without a
        value are left out). status is one of: eol (end of life, no
        support left), extended_support_only (past end of life, only extended
        or paid support left), eol_soon (end of life within warn_days),
        update_available (a newer patch exists in the cycle), supported,
        unknown_release (no cycle matches -- see the product's releases or pass
        release), unknown_product (no product or no purl match -- pass
        product), not_checked (over the budget of 50 product lookups per run --
        call again with the remaining items) or error. Versions are compared
        numerically on their leading digits ("7.2.5-alpine" = 7.2.5), and only
        when the installed and the latest version both start with the cycle
        name; otherwise update_available is left out (Java "1.8.0_392" against
        "8u514-b01", a latest of "N/A", or a version that is only the cycle
        such as Ubuntu "22.04" -- for operating systems latest is the newest
        point release, not the package patch level). days_to_eol counts from
        today (UTC), negative once past; left out when the date is unknown.
      params:
        items:
          type: array
          required: true
          description: "Array of 1-200 items: {product: 'postgresql', version: '16.4'}, {purl: 'pkg:docker/library/redis@7.2.5'}, {product: 'eclipse-temurin', version: '1.8.0_392', release: '8'}. version is optional (without it only the cycle given by release, or none, is checked)."
        warn_days:
          type: integer
          default: 90
          description: "Report eol_soon when end of life is at most this many days away (default 90)."
      timeout: 120s
      depends_on: [get_product, list_identifiers]
      code: |
        const items = params.items;
        if (!Array.isArray(items) || items.length === 0) {
          throw new Error("items must be a non-empty array of {product | purl, version?, release?}");
        }
        if (items.length > 200) {
          throw new Error("at most 200 items per call");
        }
        const warnDays = (params.warn_days === undefined || params.warn_days === null) ? 90 : Number(params.warn_days);
        const DAY = 86400000;
        const now = Date.now();
        // 50 api.* calls per composite run: one per distinct product, plus one
        // for the purl map when any item carries a purl.
        let budget = 50;

        const nn = (o) => {
          const out = {};
          Object.keys(o).forEach((k) => { if (o[k] !== null && o[k] !== undefined) out[k] = o[k]; });
          return out;
        };
        const decode = (s) => { try { return decodeURIComponent(s); } catch (e) { return s; } };
        const purlParts = (p) => {
          let s = decode(String(p || "")).trim();
          s = s.split("#")[0].split("?")[0];
          let version = null;
          const at = s.lastIndexOf("@");
          if (at > s.lastIndexOf("/")) {
            version = s.slice(at + 1);
            s = s.slice(0, at);
          }
          s = s.toLowerCase();
          // Docker images without a namespace live in "library" (purl spec).
          const m = /^pkg:docker\/([^\/]+)$/.exec(s);
          if (m) s = "pkg:docker/library/" + m[1];
          return { base: s, version: version };
        };
        const normVersion = (v) => String(v).trim().replace(/^v(?=\d)/i, "");
        const numCore = (v) => {
          const m = /^(\d+(?:\.\d+)*)/.exec(String(v || ""));
          return m ? m[1].split(".").map(Number) : null;
        };
        const cmpCore = (a, b) => {
          const n = Math.max(a.length, b.length);
          for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            const x = a[i] || 0;
            const y = b[i] || 0;
            if (x !== y) return x < y ? -1 : 1;
          }
          return 0;
        };
        const matchRelease = (releases, version) => {
          const v = normVersion(version).toLowerCase();
          let best = null;
          releases.forEach((r) => {
            const n = String(r.name).toLowerCase();
            const boundary = v.length > n.length && /[.\-_+ ]/.test(v.charAt(n.length));
            if (v === n || (v.indexOf(n) === 0 && boundary)) {
              if (!best || n.length > String(best.name).length) best = r;
            }
          });
          return best;
        };
        const daysUntil = (d) => {
          if (!d) return null;
          const t = Date.parse(String(d) + "T00:00:00Z");
          return isNaN(t) ? null : Math.ceil((t - now) / DAY);
        };
        const errText = (e) => String((e && e.message) || e);

        // purl -> product map, fetched once and only when needed.
        let purlMap = null;
        let purlMapError = null;
        const needPurls = items.some((it) => it && it.purl && !it.product);
        if (needPurls) {
          budget--;
          try {
            const ids = await api.list_identifiers({ identifier_type: "purl" });
            if (!Array.isArray(ids)) throw new Error("unexpected purl list response");
            purlMap = {};
            ids.forEach((x) => {
              const b = purlParts(x.identifier).base;
              if (!purlMap[b]) purlMap[b] = x.product;
            });
          } catch (e) {
            purlMapError = "purl lookup failed: " + errText(e);
          }
        }

        const cache = {};
        const fetchProduct = async (name) => {
          const key = String(name).toLowerCase();
          if (cache[key]) return cache[key];
          if (budget <= 0) return { skipped: true };
          budget--;
          let res;
          try {
            const p = await api.get_product({ product: name });
            if (p && typeof p === "object" && Array.isArray(p.releases)) {
              res = { product: p };
            } else {
              const t = typeof p === "string" ? p : JSON.stringify(p);
              res = { error: t, notFound: /404|not.?found/i.test(t) };
            }
          } catch (e) {
            const t = errText(e);
            res = { error: t, notFound: /404|not.?found/i.test(t) };
          }
          cache[key] = res;
          return res;
        };

        const results = [];
        for (let i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
          const it = items[i] || {};
          const entry = { index: i, query: it };
          let productName = it.product ? String(it.product).trim() : null;
          let version = (it.version !== undefined && it.version !== null && String(it.version).trim() !== "") ? String(it.version) : null;
          if (it.purl) {
            const pp = purlParts(it.purl);
            if (!version && pp.version) version = pp.version;
            if (!productName) {
              if (purlMapError) {
                entry.status = "error";
                entry.error = purlMapError;
                results.push(nn(entry));
                continue;
              }
              productName = purlMap ? purlMap[pp.base] : null;
              if (!productName) {
                entry.status = "unknown_product";
                entry.error = "no product lists the purl " + pp.base + " -- pass product (see list_products)";
                results.push(nn(entry));
                continue;
              }
            }
          }
          if (!productName) {
            entry.status = "error";
            entry.error = "item needs product or purl";
            results.push(nn(entry));
            continue;
          }

          const fp = await fetchProduct(productName);
          if (fp.skipped) {
            entry.status = "not_checked";
            entry.error = "budget of 50 product lookups per run exhausted -- call again with the remaining items";
            results.push(nn(entry));
            continue;
          }
          if (!fp.product) {
            entry.status = fp.notFound ? "unknown_product" : "error";
            entry.error = fp.notFound ? ("no product named " + productName + " -- see list_products for slugs and aliases") : fp.error;
            results.push(nn(entry));
            continue;
          }
          const p = fp.product;
          const releases = p.releases || [];
          entry.product = p.name;
          entry.product_label = p.label;

          let newest = null;
          let newestLts = null;
          releases.forEach((r) => {
            if (!newest || String(r.releaseDate || "") > String(newest.releaseDate || "")) newest = r;
            if (r.isLts && (!newestLts || String(r.releaseDate || "") > String(newestLts.releaseDate || ""))) newestLts = r;
          });
          if (newest) entry.newest_release = newest.name;
          if (newestLts) entry.newest_lts_release = newestLts.name;

          let rel = null;
          if (it.release !== undefined && it.release !== null && String(it.release) !== "") {
            const want = String(it.release).toLowerCase();
            rel = releases.find((r) => String(r.name).toLowerCase() === want) || null;
          } else if (version) {
            rel = matchRelease(releases, version);
          }
          if (version) entry.installed_version = normVersion(version);
          if (!rel) {
            entry.status = "unknown_release";
            entry.error = it.release
              ? ("product " + p.name + " has no release cycle named " + it.release)
              : (version ? ("no release cycle of " + p.name + " matches version " + version + " -- pass release") : "item needs version or release");
            entry.known_releases = releases.slice(0, 12).map((r) => r.name);
            results.push(nn(entry));
            continue;
          }

          entry.release = rel.name;
          entry.codename = rel.codename;
          entry.is_lts = rel.isLts;
          entry.release_date = rel.releaseDate;
          entry.is_eoas = rel.isEoas;
          entry.eoas_from = rel.eoasFrom;
          entry.is_eol = rel.isEol;
          entry.eol_from = rel.eolFrom;
          entry.days_to_eol = daysUntil(rel.eolFrom);
          entry.is_eoes = rel.isEoes;
          entry.eoes_from = rel.eoesFrom;
          entry.is_maintained = rel.isMaintained;
          if (rel.latest) {
            entry.latest_in_release = rel.latest.name;
            entry.latest_date = rel.latest.date;
          }
          if (newest) entry.newer_release_available = newest.name !== rel.name;

          // Compare only when the installed and the latest version both follow
          // the cycle's naming ("16.4" and "16.16" in cycle "16"); Java's
          // "1.8.0_392" against "8u514-b01" or a latest of "N/A" stay unknown.
          let update = null;
          if (version && rel.latest && rel.latest.name) {
            const iv = normVersion(version);
            const lv = normVersion(rel.latest.name);
            const inCycle = (v) => matchRelease([rel], v) !== null;
            if (iv.toLowerCase() !== String(rel.name).toLowerCase() && inCycle(iv) && inCycle(lv)) {
              const a = numCore(iv);
              const b = numCore(lv);
              if (a && b) update = cmpCore(a, b) < 0;
            }
          }
          entry.update_available = update;

          if (rel.isEol) {
            entry.status = rel.isMaintained ? "extended_support_only" : "eol";
          } else if (entry.days_to_eol !== null && entry.days_to_eol <= warnDays) {
            entry.status = "eol_soon";
          } else if (update === true) {
            entry.status = "update_available";
          } else {
            entry.status = "supported";
          }
          results.push(nn(entry));
        }

        const counts = {};
        results.forEach((r) => { counts[r.status] = (counts[r.status] || 0) + 1; });
        return { checked: items.length, status_counts: counts, results: results };

  coverage:
    endpoints: 11
    total_endpoints: 12
    percentage: 92
    focus: "endoflife.date API v1: products (EOL dates, support phases, LTS, latest versions per cycle), release cycles (EOL status by cycle name, installed-version checks with newer patches and cycles), identifiers (purl, CPE, repology lookups)."
    missing: "The API index (GET /api/v1/, a list of the top-level collections) is used only as the health check. Not covered: API v0 (/api/{product}.json, /api/{product}/{cycle}.json, /api/all.json), the legacy format the project plans to retire. The website's product pages are not part of the API."
    last_reviewed: "2026-11-25"

  setup:
    credential_steps:
      - "No credentials are required -- the endoflife.date API is public and anonymous."
      - "No signup, API key, token or OAuth client is needed for any endpoint."
      - "Keep credential: none in the DADL; ToolMesh then sends every request without authentication."
    env_var: ""
    backends_yaml: |
      - name: endoflife
        transport: rest
        dadl: endoflife.dadl
        url: "https://endoflife.date/api/v1"
    required_scopes: []
    optional_scopes: []
    docs_url: "https://endoflife.date/docs/api/v1/"
    notes: >
      Public read API of the community project endoflife.date (data and code
      under the MIT license). No authentication and no published rate limit;
      the API documents HTTP 429 with Retry-After. Base URL is
      https://endoflife.date/api/v1 . OpenAPI 3.1 spec:
      https://endoflife.date/docs/api/v1/openapi.yml , changelog:
      https://github.com/endoflife-date/endoflife.date/blob/master/CHANGELOG_API.md .
      The data is static JSON on a CDN, regenerated whenever the project's
      repository changes. Unknown product, release, category or tag names
      return HTTP 404 with an HTML page instead of a JSON error. Renamed
      products and aliases answer with a 301 redirect that ToolMesh follows.
      The health check reads the API index (about 300 bytes): with no
      credential involved it verifies reachability and the response format
      only. Corrections to the data go to the project's repository on GitHub,
      not to the API.

  examples:
    - name: "Support status of installed versions"
      description: "Check several installed products for end of life, newer patches and newer release cycles"
      code: |
        const report = await api.check_release_status({
          items: [
            { product: "ubuntu", version: "22.04.4" },
            { product: "postgresql", version: "16.4" },
            { purl: "pkg:docker/library/redis@7.2.5" },
            { product: "python", version: "3.9.20" }
          ],
          warn_days: 120
        });
        return report.results.map((r) => ({
          product: r.product,
          release: r.release,
          status: r.status,
          eol_from: r.eol_from,
          latest: r.latest_in_release,
          newest: r.newest_release
        }));
    - name: "End of life in the next 90 days across all products"
      description: "Scan the full dump inside code and return only the cycles that reach end of life soon"
      code: |
        const products = await api.list_products_full();
        const today = new Date().toISOString().slice(0, 10);
        const limit = new Date(Date.now() + 90 * 86400000).toISOString().slice(0, 10);
        const soon = [];
        products.forEach((p) => p.releases.forEach((r) => {
          if (r.eolFrom && r.eolFrom >= today && r.eolFrom <= limit) {
            soon.push({ product: p.name, release: r.name, eol: r.eolFrom });
          }
        }));
        return soon.sort((a, b) => (a.eol < b.eol ? -1 : 1));
    - name: "Newest LTS release cycle of a product"
      description: "Pick the newest cycle flagged LTS, which /releases/latest does not return"
      code: |
        const p = await api.get_product({ product: "ubuntu" });
        const lts = p.releases.filter((r) => r.isLts)
          .sort((a, b) => (a.releaseDate < b.releaseDate ? 1 : -1))[0];
        return { release: lts.name, codename: lts.codename, eol: lts.eolFrom, latest: lts.latest && lts.latest.name };
